## Solution

```c
struct Semaphore {
    int value;              // 1 = available, 0 = taken
    Queue waitingQueue;     // queue of blocked processes
};

void init(Semaphore *sem, int initialValue) {
    sem->value = initialValue;
    sem->waitingQueue = createQueue();
}

void wait(Semaphore *sem) {
    // Atomic operation
    sem->value--;

    if (sem->value < 0) {
        // Resource not available, block this process
        addToQueue(&sem->waitingQueue, currentProcess);
        block(currentProcess);  // suspend execution
    }
}

void signal(Semaphore *sem) {
    // Atomic operation
    sem->value++;

    if (sem->value <= 0) {
        // There are waiting processes
        Process *p = removeFromQueue(&sem->waitingQueue);
        wakeup(p);  // make process ready
    }
}
```

**Explanation**: The semaphore value indicates the number of available resources. When a process calls `wait()`, it decrements the value. If the value becomes negative, it means more processes are waiting than resources available, so the process blocks itself and joins the waiting queue. When `signal()` is called, the value is incremented, and if there are waiting processes (value ≤ 0), one is awakened. All operations must be atomic to prevent race conditions.

