## Solution

```matlab
function x = solve_lu(L, U, b)
    n = length(b);

    % Forward substitution: Ly = b
    y = zeros(n, 1);
    for i = 1:n
        y(i) = b(i) - L(i, 1:i-1) * y(1:i-1);
    end

    % Back-substitution: Ux = y
    x = zeros(n, 1);
    for i = n:-1:1
        x(i) = (y(i) - U(i, i+1:n) * x(i+1:n)) / U(i, i);
    end
end
```

**Explanation:**

The forward substitution loop solves each equation L(i,:)*y = b(i) for y(i). Since L has ones on the diagonal and is lower triangular, y(i) = b(i) - sum of known terms.

The back-substitution loop solves U(i,:)*x = y(i) starting from the last row. Since U is upper triangular, we can isolate x(i) by subtracting known terms (those involving x(i+1) through x(n)) and dividing by U(i,i).

