## Solution

```verilog
module sequence_detector_101 (
    input clk,
    input reset,
    input data_in,
    output sequence_found
);

    // State definitions
    localparam IDLE = 2'b00;      // Initial state
    localparam SEEN_1 = 2'b01;    // Seen first '1'
    localparam SEEN_10 = 2'b10;   // Seen '10'

    reg [1:0] current_state, next_state;

    // State transition logic (combinational)
    always @(*) begin
        case (current_state)
            IDLE: begin
                if (data_in == 1'b1)
                    next_state = SEEN_1;
                else
                    next_state = IDLE;
            end
            SEEN_1: begin
                if (data_in == 1'b0)
                    next_state = SEEN_10;
                else
                    next_state = SEEN_1;  // Stay in SEEN_1 if another 1 arrives
            end
            SEEN_10: begin
                if (data_in == 1'b1)
                    next_state = SEEN_1;  // After detecting '101', the '1' starts next sequence
                else
                    next_state = IDLE;    // Reset if we see '100'
            end
            default: next_state = IDLE;
        endcase
    end

    // Mealy output: assert when in SEEN_10 state AND data_in is 1
    assign sequence_found = (current_state == SEEN_10) && (data_in == 1'b1);

    // State register update (sequential)
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (reset)
            current_state <= IDLE;
        else
            current_state <= next_state;
    end

endmodule
```

**Explanation:** The FSM has three states tracking progress through "101". Transitions occur based on input bits. The output is combinational (Mealy-style): `sequence_found` is asserted only when the current state is SEEN_10 AND the new input is '1'. Critically, upon detection, the next state is SEEN_1 (not IDLE), enabling overlapping sequence detection.

